# Coach speech: the review of the player's moves (src/coach/review.cpp, review_explain.cpp).
# English is the reference language. Same syntax as assets/i18n: "key = value", '#' comments.
#
# Translators, read this first:
# - "key", "key.2", "key.3" are alternative phrasings of the same message; keep the same number.
# - ".b1" ... ".b6" at the end of a key is the coach level the line is for (1 = beginner, gentle
#   and simple; 6 = expert, terse). Keep the tone of each level.
# - Placeholders are filled by the program. The coach points at the board while it says them, so
#   every placeholder of the English line must stay in your line (word order is free):
#     {your} {your2}   one of the listener's pieces, WITH its possessive: "your knight"
#     {my} {my2}       one of the coach's pieces, with its possessive: "my bishop"
#     {t1} {t2}        pieces under attack (anyone's), with their possessive: "your queen"
#     {sq}             a square: "e4"
#     {move}           the player's move; {best} a better move; {reply} the coach's answer
#     {line}           a few moves in a row: "Nxe5 dxe5 Qg4"
#     {n} {n2} {pts} {m}  numbers; "{pts:point}" says "3 points", "{m:move}" says "2 moves"
#                     (the counted nouns are in common.lang, count.<noun>)
#     {eval}           an evaluation from the player's side: "+1.3", "mate in 4"
#     {text}           a name from this file (name.pattern.*)
#     {theme}          a phrase from appraisal.lang: theme.* ("watching for double attacks")
#   Never write "your" or "my" before {your}, {my}, {t1}, {t2}: the possessive is already in them.
# - Lines at levels 1-2 must stay short (at most about 15 words per sentence).

# ---- Announcements (either side's move) -------------------------------------------------------
# The player gives check (levels 1-2: say what it means).
ann.check.human.b1 = Check! My king is attacked.
ann.check.human.b1.2 = Check! You are attacking my king.
ann.check.human.b1.3 = That's check! My king has to get out of it.
ann.check.human = Check.
ann.check.human.2 = Check!
ann.check.human.3 = And that's check.
# The coach gives check.
ann.check.coach = Check!
ann.check.coach.2 = Check.
ann.check.coach.3 = And check!
# Once per game at levels 1-2, right after the coach's first check.
ann.check.coach.first = That means {your} is attacked. You must get it out of check.
ann.check.coach.first.2 = Check means {your} is under attack. Move it, block, or capture my piece.
ann.double_check.coach = Double check! Two of my pieces attack your king at once, so it must move.
ann.double_check.coach.2 = That's a double check: only a king move helps you now.
ann.double_check.human = Double check! Two of your pieces attack my king at once, so it must move.
ann.double_check.human.2 = A double check! Only a king move can save me now.
# The player checkmates the coach.
ann.mate.human.b1 = Checkmate! You won! My king can't escape.
ann.mate.human.b1.2 = That's checkmate! Well done, you won the game!
ann.mate.human.b1.3 = Checkmate! My king is trapped. Congratulations!
ann.mate.human = Checkmate. Well played.
ann.mate.human.2 = That's mate. Congratulations.
ann.mate.human.3 = Checkmate! Nicely done.
# The coach checkmates the player.
ann.mate.coach.b1 = That's checkmate. Your king has no way out.
ann.mate.coach.b1.2 = Checkmate. Your king is attacked and cannot escape.
ann.mate.coach.b1.3 = I'm afraid that's checkmate. Your king can't get away.
ann.mate.coach = Checkmate.
ann.mate.coach.2 = And that's mate.
ann.mate.coach.3 = Checkmate, I'm afraid.

